---
import ContactForm from '@components/ContactForm.astro'
import { SitePage } from '@data/analyticsCaller'
import Grid from '@components/Grid.astro'
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro'
import CaseStudySectionText from '@components/case-studies/case-study/CaseStudySectionText.astro'
import CaseStudyResults from '@components/case-studies/case-study/CaseStudyResults.astro'
import CaseStudyHeader from '@components/case-studies/case-study/CaseStudyHeader.astro'
import CaseStudyToggleButton from '@components/case-studies/case-study/CaseStudyToggleButton.astro'
import CaseStudyMetaData from '@components/case-studies/case-study/CaseStudyMetaData.astro'
import CaseStudyShout from '@components/case-studies/case-study/CaseStudyShout.astro'
import CaseStudyNav from '@components/case-studies/case-study/CaseStudyNav.astro'
import CaseStudyImages from '@components/case-studies/case-study/CaseStudyImages.astro'
import { getCaseStudyNav } from '@data/caseStudies'

import hero from '@assets/case-studies/sonata-ai/hero.webp'
import squareOne from '@assets/case-studies/sonata-ai/squareOne.svg'
import squareTwo from '@assets/case-studies/sonata-ai/squareTwo.webp'
import wideOne from '@assets/case-studies/sonata-ai/wideOne.webp'
import wideTwo from '@assets/case-studies/sonata-ai/wideTwo.webp'

const { previous, next } = getCaseStudyNav('sonata-ai')
---

<Layout
  title="Sonata AI Acoustic Risk Calculator Case Study"
  description="AI for Sonata, a Fletcher Building brand of acoustic panels for commercial fit-outs and interiors. Pixel Agency built a custom AI-powered Acoustic Risk Tool, a CatBoost prediction model that estimates acoustic risk from early design inputs, giving architects and designers an instant read at the point decisions are made and positioning Sonata as the brand that helps them see the problem before it becomes one."
>
  <article id="case-study-page">
    <CaseStudyHeader>
      <span slot="title">Sonata AI Acoustic Risk Calculator</span>
      <span slot="industry">Construction, Manufacturing</span>
      <span slot="services">AI</span>
      <span slot="duration">1 month</span>
    </CaseStudyHeader>

    <CaseStudyToggleButton />

    <!-- Single shell: all images + summary (left pane) / all case study text (right pane) -->
    <section class="pb-12 lg:pb-18">
      <Grid>
        <div id="case-study-shell" class="case-study-shell col-span-full">
          <div class="case-study-left-pane min-w-0 overflow-hidden">
            <CaseStudyImages
              heroImage={{ src: hero }}
              summary="How Pixel Agency built Sonata an AI-powered Acoustic Risk Tool, giving architects and designers an instant read on whether a space is heading for an acoustic problem and putting Sonata in the conversation early."
              squareImages={[{ src: squareOne }, { src: squareTwo }]}
              wideImages={[{ src: wideOne }, { src: wideTwo }]}
            />
          </div>

          <div class="case-study-right-pane min-w-0">
            <CaseStudyShout> Helping architects spot acoustic risk before the design is locked in. </CaseStudyShout>

            <CaseStudySectionText title="The client" noTopMargin={true}>
              <p>
                Sonata is a Fletcher Building brand of acoustic panels designed for commercial fit-outs, offices,
                education spaces, and interiors where acoustic performance matters. Sonata sells through architects,
                designers, and specifiers, the people who shape a space long before a panel goes on a wall. Getting
                Sonata specified means being part of the conversation early, when the acoustic risk is still a question
                rather than a problem.
              </p>
            </CaseStudySectionText>

            <CaseStudySectionText title="The problem">
              <p>
                Acoustic risk is one of those things that's hard to see until it's too late. A room can look right on a
                plan and still sound wrong once it's built. Architects and designers don't always have an easy way to
                assess the acoustic risk of a space at the moment they're making the decisions that drive it: room size,
                ceiling height, surface character, occupancy density, glazing, floor type. By the time the room is in
                use, the problem (and the cost of fixing it) is locked in.
              </p><br />
              <p>
                Sonata's marketing team saw an opportunity in that gap. If Sonata could give architects and designers a
                quick, credible way to assess acoustic risk early, Sonata would stop being just a product to specify and
                start being a useful tool in the design process itself. The challenge was turning that idea into
                something real, technically credible, and trusted by a professional audience.
              </p>
            </CaseStudySectionText>

            <CaseStudySectionText title="The approach">
              <p>
                Sonata brought the idea to us as a differentiator they wanted to validate. Our job was to take the
                concept from "this could work" to "this does work" by building a working prototype that proved out the
                technical approach and gave Sonata something tangible to show stakeholders. We started by working with
                Sonata to define the inputs an architect would actually have at the early design stage, the outputs that
                would be useful at that moment, and the level of confidence the tool needed to project to be credible to
                a professional audience.
              </p><br />
              <p>
                From there we designed the system around a custom AI prediction model: a CatBoost gradient boosting
                model trained on the relationship between room characteristics and acoustic risk outcomes. CatBoost was
                chosen because it handles the structured, tabular nature of the design inputs well, performs strongly on
                smaller training datasets, and produces calibrated confidence scores rather than the black-box guesses
                you sometimes get from off-the-shelf AI.
              </p>
            </CaseStudySectionText>

            <CaseStudySectionText title="The solution">
              <p>
                The Acoustic Risk Tool is built as a self-contained, Sonata-branded application that is designed to
                embed cleanly inside the Sonata website. The architecture is deliberately lightweight: a FastAPI and
                Pydantic backend serving the trained CatBoost model, a Sonata-branded front end for architects and
                designers, and a single dedicated server hosting the model and API. No third-party AI services in the
                loop, no data leaving Sonata's environment unnecessarily, and full control over the model and its
                training.
              </p><br />
              <p>
                A user steps through a short, considered set of inputs: space type, room size, etc., the model returns
                an acoustic risk band (Low, Medium, or High), a confidence score, and the factors that contributed most
                to the result, so the answer isn't just a number but a starting point for a design conversation. Sonata
                products are surfaced as part of the response where appropriate, putting the brand in front of
                architects at the exact moment they're thinking about how to bring the risk down.
              </p>
            </CaseStudySectionText>

            <CaseStudySectionText title="The results">
              <p>
                The tool is currently in pre-launch, with a working demonstration validated end-to-end and the model
                trained on representative scenarios:
              </p>
              <CaseStudyResults
                results={[
                  {
                    title: 'A working AI tool',
                    description:
                      'demonstrated end-to-end with the trained CatBoost model returning calibrated risk bands, confidence scores, and contributing factors',
                    backgroundColour: 'pixel-solar'
                  },
                  {
                    title: 'A custom architecture',
                    description:
                      'that gives Sonata full control over the model, the data, and the training process, with no dependence on third-party AI services',
                    backgroundColour: 'pixel-celestial'
                  },
                  {
                    title: 'A Sonata-branded experience',
                    description:
                      'designed to embed inside the Sonata website via a secure iframe, ready to drop into the live site with no disruption',
                    backgroundColour: 'pixel-rosy'
                  }
                ]}
              />

              <div class="mt-6 lg:mt-12"></div>

              <CaseStudyShout>
                An acoustic problem is cheap to fix before the room is built. After that, it's not. AI gives us the
                power to see the risk before it's a problem.
              </CaseStudyShout>
            </CaseStudySectionText>

            <CaseStudyMetaData
              client={{ name: 'Sonata', url: 'https://sonataacousticpanels.com.au/' }}
              industry={['Construction', 'Manufacturing']}
              serviceNames={['AI']}
            />
          </div>
        </div>
      </Grid>
    </section>

    {/* Navigation section */}
    <CaseStudyNav previousCaseStudy={previous} nextCaseStudy={next} />

    <ContactForm
      title="Discuss a similar challenge"
      displayDescription={false}
      caller={SitePage.CASE_STUDIES_SONATA_AI}
    />
  </article>

  <style>
    #case-study-page {
      --case-study-border: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-black) 8%, transparent);
      --case-study-placeholder: var(--color-pixel-placeholder-grey);
    }

    @media (max-width: 1023px) {
      .case-study-shell {
        display: flex;
        gap: 0;
        overflow: clip;
      }

      .case-study-left-pane {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        width: 100%;
        max-height: 5000px;
        opacity: 1;
        transform: translateX(0);
        transition:
          width 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
          max-height 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
          opacity 240ms ease,
          transform 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
        will-change: width, max-height, opacity, transform;
      }

      #case-study-page[data-panel-state='open'] .case-study-left-pane {
        width: 0;
        max-height: 0;
        opacity: 0;
        transform: translateX(-2rem);
        pointer-events: none;
      }

      .case-study-right-pane {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        width: 0;
        max-height: 0;
        opacity: 0;
        overflow: clip;
        transform: translateX(2rem);
        transition:
          width 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
          max-height 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
          opacity 260ms ease,
          transform 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
        will-change: width, max-height, opacity, transform;
      }

      #case-study-page[data-panel-state='open'] .case-study-right-pane {
        width: 100%;
        max-height: 5000px;
        opacity: 1;
        transform: translateX(0);
        pointer-events: auto;
      }
    }

    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .case-study-shell {
        display: flex;
        gap: 0;
        transition: gap 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
      }

      #case-study-page[data-panel-state='open'] .case-study-shell {
        gap: 1.25rem;
      }

      .case-study-left-pane,
      .case-study-right-pane {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        transition:
          width 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
          max-height 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
          opacity 260ms ease,
          transform 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
        will-change: width, max-height, opacity, transform;
      }

      .case-study-left-pane {
        width: 100%;
        max-height: 5000px;
      }

      .case-study-right-pane {
        width: 0;
        max-height: 0;
        opacity: 0;
        transform: translateX(2rem);
        overflow: clip;
        pointer-events: none;
      }

      #case-study-page[data-panel-state='open'] .case-study-left-pane {
        width: calc(50% - 0.625rem);
      }

      #case-study-page[data-panel-state='open'] .case-study-right-pane {
        width: calc(50% - 0.625rem);
        max-height: 5000px;
        opacity: 1;
        transform: translateX(0);
        pointer-events: auto;
      }
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .case-study-shell,
      .case-study-left-pane,
      .case-study-right-pane {
        transition: none;
      }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    function initCaseStudyPage() {
      const page = document.getElementById('case-study-page')
      const toggle = document.getElementById('case-study-toggle')
      if (!(page instanceof HTMLElement) || !(toggle instanceof HTMLButtonElement)) return
      const pageElement = page
      const toggleButton = toggle

      const mobileMedia = window.matchMedia('(max-width: 1023px)')

      function setToggleTextColor() {
        const accent = getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--accent-bgc').trim()
        const match = accent.match(/(\d+)\s*,\s*(\d+)\s*,\s*(\d+)/)
        if (!match) return

        let red = parseInt(match[1], 10) / 255
        let green = parseInt(match[2], 10) / 255
        let blue = parseInt(match[3], 10) / 255

        red = red <= 0.03928 ? red / 12.92 : Math.pow((red + 0.055) / 1.055, 2.4)
        green = green <= 0.03928 ? green / 12.92 : Math.pow((green + 0.055) / 1.055, 2.4)
        blue = blue <= 0.03928 ? blue / 12.92 : Math.pow((blue + 0.055) / 1.055, 2.4)

        const luminance = 0.2126 * red + 0.7152 * green + 0.0722 * blue
        document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--case-study-toggle-text', luminance > 0.4 ? '#000' : '#fff')
      }

      function getDefaultState() {
        return 'closed'
      }

      if (!pageElement.dataset.panelState) {
        pageElement.dataset.panelState = getDefaultState()
      }

      function syncA11y() {
        const isOpen = pageElement.dataset.panelState === 'open'
        toggleButton.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(isOpen))
      }

      setToggleTextColor()
      syncA11y()

      toggleButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
        pageElement.dataset.panelState = pageElement.dataset.panelState === 'open' ? 'closed' : 'open'
        syncA11y()
      })
    }

    initCaseStudyPage()
    document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', initCaseStudyPage)
  </script>
</Layout>
